  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 152 AM MDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nA similar forecast remains in place for Sunday, as the center of the \nupper high slides a tad south and east across the southeast plains. \nThis will bring another few degrees of warming to southeast Colorado, \nwith highs expected on the upper 90s to possibly 109F across the far\nsoutheast plains. With that said, we have added Crowley and Baca counties\nto the current Heat Advisory in place for Sunday. Could also see a few\nrecords fall on Sunday, with current record highs being 91F for ALS in \n2023, 97F for COS in 1953, and 103F for PUB in 2023. As for convection, \nmodel data continues to indicate enough residual moisture in place, to \nwork with strong solar heating to support scattered showers and storms \nover the higher terrain once again on Sunday. Weak westerly flow will \nhelp to push storms east across the high mtn valleys and immediate adjacent\nplains through the afternoon and evening hours. Again, will need to watch \nfor potential flash flooding for recent burn areas.  \n\nFor next week, weak westerly flow will continue to increase monsoonal \nmoisture across all of south central and southeast Colorado, leading to a\nslow cooling and wetting trend across the area. The southeast plains will \nat least get some chances of showers and storms through the middle and end \nof next week, with abundant moisture in place. The daily rounds of slow \nmoving showers and storms will raise flash flooding concerns over the burn \nscars, as well as areas that develop saturated soils due to the increase \nin daily storms.",
